# Camino - Cursor Rules
# Last updated: 7 Feb 2026

## Project Overview

Camino is a mobile-first business intelligence platform that turns raw business data (CSV uploads, integrations) into actionable signals with AI-powered interpretation. The target user is a small business executive/manager who needs to understand their data without being a data analyst.

North Star metric: 1 upload (manager) + 1 view/week (any user)

Current phase: MVP Tier 1 - getting the core pipeline to "start charging" state.


## Tech Stack

- Framework: Next.js 16 (App Router) with React 19.2
- Language: TypeScript 5
- Styling: Tailwind CSS v4 with shadcn/ui components
- Database: Supabase (PostgreSQL with RLS)
- AI: Vercel AI SDK v6 (ai@6.0.6)
- File parsing: xlsx for Excel/CSV
- Hosting: Vercel
- Icons: lucide-react (^0.454.0)
- Forms: react-hook-form + zod
- Charts: Recharts via shadcn/ui chart components
- Date utils: date-fns 4.1.0


## Architecture

### Route Groups
- `app/(protected)/*` - Customer-facing pages (auth-guarded, mobile-first, bottom nav)
- `app/admin/*` - Admin panel (sidebar nav, desktop layout)
- `app/auth/*` - Authentication flows (login, signup, onboarding)
- `app/api/*` - API routes (REST endpoints)
- `app/test/*` - Test/debug pages (smoke tests, e2e, review)

### Supabase Clients
There are TWO Supabase client files. Use the correct one:
- `lib/supabase/client.ts` - Browser/client components. Uses `createBrowserClient()`. Call in 'use client' components.
- `lib/supabase/server.ts` - Server components and API routes. Uses `createServerClient()` with cookies. Always `await createClient()`.
- `lib/supabase/admin.ts` - Service role client for admin operations. Uses SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY.

### Key Services (lib/)
- `upload-service.ts` - CSV upload processing
- `staging-service.ts` - Data staging before signal creation
- `signal-discovery-service.ts` - AI-powered signal detection from staged data
- `signal-calculation-service.ts` - Statistical calculations (sum, avg, trend, change%)
- `interpretation-service.ts` - AI interpretation (5-section analysis: what_we_found, what_it_means, so_what)
- `signals-service.ts` - CRUD operations for signals table
- `organization-service.ts` - Organization management
- `admin-service.ts` - Admin user management, password resets
- `csv-parser.ts` / `xlsx-parser.ts` - File parsing utilities

### Critical Pipeline (Tier 1 MVP)
This is the core value loop. Every change to these files needs extra care:

```
Upload CSV (upload-page-client.tsx)
  -> POST /api/upload (route.ts) - parses file, stages data
  -> Signal Discovery (signal-discovery-service.ts) - AI identifies signals
  -> POST /api/upload/calculate (route.ts) - statistical calculations
  -> Signal Calculation (signal-calculation-service.ts) - computes values
  -> AI Interpretation (interpretation-service.ts) - generates 5-section analysis
  -> Display (signals-page-client.tsx, signal-detail-client.tsx) - signal cards + expanded view
```


## Database

### Environments
- Production: `fimcowyogwffkpckicjl` (supabase-orange-yacht) - customer-facing
- Development: `juacwfiquhzawpzaylen` (camino-dev) - testing and development

For local dev, use .env.local pointing at camino-dev:
```
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://juacwfiquhzawpzaylen.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=<camino-dev anon key>
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=<camino-dev service role key>
```

### Core Tables (18 total)
- profiles - User profiles, linked to auth.users
- organizations - Customer organizations
- organization_members - User-org membership with roles (admin/read-only)
- signals - Discovered signals with values, trends, status
- data_points - Time-series data for signals (signal_id, date, value)
- signal_interpretations - Cached AI interpretations (what_we_found, what_it_means, so_what)
- saved_signals - User bookmarks
- signal_shares - Share tracking
- kpi_ownership - Which user owns which signal
- decisions - Business decisions linked to signals
- decision_signals - Junction table for decisions-signals
- benchmark_history - Historical benchmark values
- data_quality_metrics - Completeness/timeliness/accuracy scores
- signal_analytics - Pre-computed analytics per period
- signal_alerts - Threshold breaches, missing data alerts
- upload_history - Upload tracking (file_name, status, signals_created)
- integrations - OAuth connections (zoho-crm, zoho-desk, hubspot)
- sync_history - Integration sync tracking

### RLS Pattern
All tables have Row Level Security enabled. Common patterns:
- Profiles: users can only see/edit their own profile
- Signals: open read for all authenticated users, authenticated write
- Organizations: members can read, admins can update
- Upload history: users can only see their own uploads

### Migration Discipline
1. Write SQL in `/scripts/` with descriptive name
2. Run against camino-dev first
3. Test the app against dev
4. Run the EXACT SAME file against prod (no freehand SQL)
5. Commit the script to git


## Coding Conventions

### General
- Use TypeScript strict mode
- Prefer `const` over `let`
- Use async/await, never raw promises
- Error handling: always try/catch in API routes, return meaningful JSON errors
- No console.log in production code (use console.error for actual errors)

### Components
- Split pages into page.tsx (server) + *-client.tsx (client component)
- Use shadcn/ui components from `@/components/ui/*`
- Import utility: `import { cn } from "@/lib/utils"`
- Icons from lucide-react only
- Mobile-first: design for 375px width, then scale up

### API Routes
- Always validate input with zod or manual checks
- Always check auth: `const supabase = await createClient()` then `supabase.auth.getUser()`
- Return consistent JSON: `{ success: boolean, data?: any, error?: string }`
- Use parameterized queries (Supabase handles this via its client)

### Styling
- Tailwind CSS v4 with design tokens (bg-background, text-foreground, etc.)
- Never use direct colors (no bg-white, text-black) - use semantic tokens
- Fonts: Geist (sans) and Geist Mono (mono) via --font-sans and --font-mono
- Spacing: use Tailwind scale (p-4, gap-6) not arbitrary values
- Mobile breakpoints: default is mobile, use md: and lg: for larger screens

### Protected Pages Pattern
```tsx
// app/(protected)/example/page.tsx (server component, thin wrapper)
import { ExampleClient } from "@/components/example-client"
export default function ExamplePage() {
  return <ExampleClient />
}

// components/example-client.tsx (client component, all logic)
"use client"
import { createBrowserClient } from "@/lib/supabase/client"
export function ExampleClient() {
  const supabase = createBrowserClient()
  // ... component logic
}
```

### Admin Pages Pattern
Admin pages can be client components directly in the page file since the admin layout already handles auth:
```tsx
// app/admin/example/page.tsx
"use client"
import { createBrowserClient } from "@/lib/supabase/client"
export default function AdminExamplePage() {
  const supabase = createBrowserClient()
  // ... admin logic
}
```


## What NOT To Do

- Do NOT use localStorage for data persistence
- Do NOT add new npm packages without checking if an existing one covers the need
- Do NOT modify the Supabase client files without understanding both client/server patterns
- Do NOT run migrations directly on prod - always dev first
- Do NOT build features outside the current sprint scope (check /docs/delivery-plan.ts)
- Do NOT add purple/violet colors (design system uses blue primary + neutral accents)
- Do NOT use `space-*` Tailwind classes - use `gap-*` instead
- Do NOT create new service files when existing ones cover the domain


